  "Question": "For which of the following values of \\(k\\) does the equation \\[\\dfrac{x - 1}{x - 2} = \\dfrac{x - k}{x - 6}\\] have no solution for \\(x?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(4\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The denominators require \\(x\\ne2\\) and \\(x\\ne6\\). For these allowed values, multiplying by \\((x-2)(x-6)\\) is reversible and gives\n\\[\n(x-1)(x-6)=(x-k)(x-2).\n\\]\nExpanding both sides makes the \\(x^2\\) terms cancel:\n\\[\nx^2-7x+6=x^2-(k+2)x+2k,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n(k-5)x=2k-6.\n\\]\nIf \\(k=5\\), this becomes \\(0=4\\), which is impossible. Therefore choice E gives no solution.\n\nTo check the other choices, when \\(k\\ne5\\) the only candidate is\n\\[\nx=\\frac{2k-6}{k-5}.\n\\]\nFor \\(k=1,2,3,4\\), respectively, this gives \\(x=1,\\frac23,0,-2\\). None equals \\(2\\) or \\(6\\), so each is allowed and satisfies the original equation by the reversible steps above.\n\nThus the requested value is \\(\\boxed{k=5}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
